## Updated Processing for the HCP-YA 2025 Release

## MR Structural Pipelines

### PreFreeSurfer pipeline
The main goals of the first structural pipeline, PreFreeSurfer, are to produce an undistorted “native” structural volume space for each subject, align the T1w and T2w images, perform a B1- (receive bias field) correction, and register the subject’s native structural volume space to MNI space. Thus, there are two volume spaces in HCP data: 1) The subject’s undistorted native volume space (rigidly “ACPC aligned” to the axes of MNI space), which is where volumes and areas of structures should be measured and where tractography should be performed, as this space is the best approximation of the subject’s physical brain. 2) Standard MNI space is useful for comparisons across subjects and studies, particularly of subcortical data, which is more accurately aligned by nonlinear volume registration than is cortical data. Note, however, that MNI space is biased to be larger than the typical individual’s brain. 
Steps:
1.	Gradient distortion correction.
2.	ACPC (i.e., 6 degrees of freedom) registration for T1w and T2w (FLIRT plus a customized script).  The acpc registered distortion corrected T1w image is in the “native” volume space.
3.	Initial brain extraction for T1w and T2w (FNIRT-atlas-based-brain-mask).
4.	Coregistration of T1w and T2w using a customized FLIRT BBR algorithm.
5.	Bias field correction using sqrt(T1w X T2w) (Rilling et al., 2012).
6.	Atlas registration (FLIRT linear + FNIRT nonlinear to MNI152).

#### Note: Difference between HCP-YA vs. Lifespan data: 
Readout distortion correction was performed in HCP-YA in stage #4 but could not be done in HCP Lifespan due to the multi-echo MPRAGE using different readout directions for different echoes.  The inputs to this pipeline were customized as necessary to get subjects successfully processed through the pipeline as described above in the QC code section.  

### FreeSurfer pipeline
The FreeSurfer pipeline for Lifespan HCP is based on a custom version of FreeSurfer 6.0, which is another difference from HCP-YA, which used FreeSurfer 5.3.0-HCP. We currently recommend running HCP pipelines with FreeSurfer 6.0 because initial testing showed significant regressions in surface placement performance in FreeSurfer 7.X. The main goals of this pipeline are to segment the volume into predefined structures (including the subcortical parcels used in CIFTI), reconstruct white and pial cortical surfaces, and perform FreeSurfer’s standard folding-based surface registration to their surface atlas (fsaverage). 
A custom version of FreeSurfer’s recon-all, called recon-all.v6.hires, with modifications of the v6.0 recon-all to better handle hires and T2 surface placement was used. The changes include:
1. adding ability to specify a mask for mri_em_register
2. Running the standard 1mm recon-all stream up until the final white and pial surfaces are created. At that point, the script conf2hires is run. This script places the 1mm surfaces on the hires input (including T2 pial placement) and maps the surface coordinates back into the conformed space to generate the final white and pial surfaces. At that point, the standard recon-all stream is run until completion. To run with this facility, add the -conf2hires flag. Do not use -hires or -cubic or -cm.
3. Changes to make it compatible with the longitudinal stream
Notes:
- No binaries outside of the FreeSurfer version 6 distribution are needed.
- Can run with 3D FLAIR instead of T2w if desired.

### PostFreeSurfer pipeline
The final structural pipeline, PostFreeSurfer, produces all of the GIFTI surface and auxiliary files necessary for viewing the data in Connectome Workbench, performs individual surface registration using cortical folding surface features and the MSM algorithm (MSMSulc), downsampling registered surfaces for connectivity analysis, creating the final brain mask, and creating myelin maps. 

Instead of using the FreeSurfer spherical surface rotation and warping for intersubject registration, we extract only the surface rotation component from FreeSurfer and use that as the initial input into the novel multimodal surface matching (MSM) algorithm (Robinson et al. 2018). Individual cortical folding surface features (FreeSurfer’s ‘sulc’ measure of average convexity) are used with the FreeSurfer spherical surface in MSMSulc to perform individual surface registration to a group-average surface template.  MSMSulc registration is “gentler” in that it introduces less distortion, performing substantially better in distortion and slightly better in cross-subject alignment of brain areas and brain function than FreeSurfer cortical folding registration alone. In addition, MSMSulc served as a precursor to more substantial improvements available from MSM registration using the information from myelin maps and rfMRI correlations together (MSMAll), described below. 
 
There are three surface spaces in HCP data: the native surface mesh for each individual (~136k vertices, most accurate for volume to surface mapping), the high resolution FS_LR registered standard mesh (~164k vertices, appropriate for cross-subject analysis of high resolution data like myelin maps) and the low resolution FS_LR registered standard mesh (~32k vertices, appropriate for cross-subject analysis of low resolution data like fMRI or diffusion). These spaces are also shown in Figure 8 Glasser et al. 2013. The 91,282 standard grayordinates (CIFTI) space is made up of a standard subcortical segmentation in 2mm MNI space (from the Conte69 subjects) and the 32k FS_LR mesh of both hemispheres (Figure 1 Glasser et al. 2013). *All HCP Version 4+ pipelines, including the structural pipeline, output CIFTI-2.0 format files.
Steps:
1.	Creation of Connectome Workbench data files and spec files, creation of FreeSurfer segmentation brain mask. 
2.	Surface registration with MSMSulc and downsampling, volume transformation of surfaces nonlinearly into MNI space. Creation of 2mm grayordinates space.  
3.	Creation of FreeSurfer ribbon files with full 0.8mm resolution data (masks of grey matter and white matter).
4.	Myelin mapping and combination of all transforms for one step resampling of T1w and T2w images from original to MNI space (Glasser and Van Essen, 2011). “_BC” intensity normalization of myelin maps (Glasser et al. 2013).  
Following completion of the structural pipelines, the functional or diffusion pipelines may run.

#### Note: Difference between HCP-YA vs. Lifespan data: 
MSMSulc was updated to use strain-based regularization instead of the pairwise angular deviations used in HCP-YA (Robinson et al., 2018 vs Robinson et al., 2014).

## MR Functional Pipelines
### fMRIVolume pipeline 
The first functional pipeline, fMRIVolume, removes spatial distortions, realigns volumes to compensate for subject motion, registers the fMRI data to the structural, reduces the bias field, normalizes the 4D image to a global mean, and masks the data with the final brain mask. Standard volume-based analyses of the fMRI data could proceed from the output of this pipeline. Care is taken in the fMRIVolume pipeline to minimize the smoothing from interpolation, and no overt volume smoothing is done. No temporal preprocessing is done.  
Steps:
1.	Gradient distortion correction.
2.	MCFLIRT-based motion correction using the “SBRef” volume as the target (Jenkinson et al. 2002).
3.	TOPUP-based field map preprocessing using Spin echo field map (for each day of each BOLD run) 
4.	Distortion correction and EPI to T1w registration of the “SBRef” volume using a customized FLIRT BBR algorithm for distortion correction and bbregister to fine tune the registration. A Jacobian file representing the intensity modulations of the distortion correction is applied to the data. 
5.	One step spline resampling from the original EPI frames to atlas space including all transforms (motion, EPI distortion, EPI to T1w from FLIRT BBR, fine tuning of EPI to T1w with bbregister, nonlinear T1w to MNI).
6.	Intensity normalization to mean of 10000 (like in FEAT) and bias field removal based on the relationship between the spin echo field map and the SBRef images.  The brain mask was based on the FreeSurfer segmentation. 
Note: Difference between HCP-YA vs. Lifespan data: 
For 3T HCP-YA data, the bias field correction that was applied to the fMRI scans as part of the minimal processing was estimated from the T1w and T2w anatomical scans as described above (known as the “LEGACY” method in the GenericfMRIVolumeProcessingPipeline.sh script).  For Lifespan HCP (and 7T HCP-YA) data, we used a better method for the bias field correction of the fMRI scans, in which the coil bias field was estimated from the “SpinEchoFieldMap” scans acquired within the same scan session as the fMRI (and applied using --biascorrection=SEBASED in the GenericfMRIVolumeProcessingPipeline.sh script). The Jacobian is now applied to the fMRI data. 

### fMRISurface pipeline
The main goal of the second functional pipeline, fMRISurface, is to bring the timeseries from the volume into the CIFTI grayordinates standard space. This is accomplished by mapping the voxels within the cortical grey matter ribbon onto the native cortical surface, transforming them according to the MSMSulc surface registration onto the 32k FS_LR mesh, and mapping the set of subcortical grey matter voxels from each subcortical parcel in each individual to a standard set of voxels in each atlas parcel. The result is a standard set of grayordinates in every subject (i.e. the same number in each subject, and with spatial correspondence) with 2mm average surface vertex and subcortical volume voxel spacing. These data are smoothed with surface (novel algorithm, see Glasser et al. 2013) and parcel constrained smoothing of 2mm FWHM to regularize the mapping process. The output of these pipelines is a CIFTI dense timeseries that can be used for subsequent resting-state or task fMRI analyses. 
Steps:
1.	Cortical ribbon-based volume to surface mapping (from MNI space 2mm volume to MNI space native mesh surface).
1.	Voxels between white and pial surface are included in mapping.  Voxels that are only partially between white and pial surface are weighted according to their partial volume that is between the two surfaces.
2.	Voxels with a temporal coefficient of variance greater than 0.5 standard deviations of their local neighborhood (sigma=5mm) are excluded from volume to surface mapping.  The practical effect of this is to remove any small vessels and brain rim voxels remaining in the ribbon.
3.	Transformation of timeseries from native mesh to fs_LR registered 32k mesh (2mm average vertex spacing) in a single step.
2.	Surface-based smoothing (2mm FWHM).
3.	Subcortical parcel-constrained smoothing and atlas resampling (2mm FWHM).
4.	Creation of dense timeseries (on a standard set of grayordinates).

### sICA-FIX pipeline
A variety of confounding and nuisance signals are present in the fMRI timeseries, particularly those arising from subject motion and subject physiology, such as respiration and heart rate. If not removed systematically, such signals can lead to false positives, false negatives, and erroneous interpretations of fMRI results. Automated methods have been developed for selectively removing spatially specific structured temporal noise—i.e., non-random time-varying artifacts that are also spatially nonuniform—using spatial independent component analysis (sICA, e.g., sICA + FIX, FMRIB's ICA component classifier (Griffanti et al., 2014)). The sICA + FIX used by HCP separates spatially specific neural signal and structured noise by decomposing fMRI data into spatially orthogonal independent components (using sICA) (Salimi-Khorshidi et al 2014). sICA-FIX was modified to clean multiple fMRI runs at a time after Functional Preprocessing (Glasser et al. 2018; 2019). This multirun sICA-FIX (MR FIX) pipeline utilizes concatenation across runs and phase encoding directions within a single visit and is especially useful for cleaning shorter runs because ICA works best with many samples (voxels or timepoints). Before concatenation, the mean image is removed from the data, linear trends are removed, and the unstructured noise is made equal across runs via variance normalization. The sICA + FIX (MR FIX) pipeline is run after Functional Preprocessing and results in the cleaned grayordinate-wise (“dense”) timeseries or voxelwise timeseries.  After cleanup, the data are split back into runs and the variance normalization is reverted (per run for individual runs or on average for concatenated data) and final results are presented in intensity bias corrected form (a given %BOLD deviation means the same thing across the whole brain). After MR FIX, a semi-automated approach was used to refine the sICA component classification called “_rclean.” Importantly, the artifact sICA components are regressed out of the data non-aggressively (i.e., modeling the neural signal and artifact variance separately) so as to selectively clean the data of artifacts without modifying the neural signal.  Note, that sICA+FIX is a selective approach (affecting only artifacts and not affecting the neural signal) that renders non-selective denoising approaches such as movement regressors, tissue-based regressors like CSF or WM, bandpass temporal filtering, and scrubbing duplicative.  It is not recommended to process HCP data with these methods.  

#### Note: Difference between HCP-YA vs. Lifespan data: 
In HCP-YA preprocessing for the 3T data (available March 2013-July 2025), single run sICA+FIX was performed for the resting state fMRI data.  For the 7T data, single run sICA+FIX was performed for the resting state and movie fMRI data.  MR+FIX was performed for the retinotopic data.  This means that the 3T task fMRI data *were not* cleaned with sICA+FIX.  Additionally, movement regressors were regressed out of the HCP-YA data; however, subsequently this was found to be deleterious (Glasser et al., 2019).  We have subsequently updated the HCP-YA data to include SEBased bias field correction, to not regress out movement regressors, MR+FIX for 3T task fMRI data, and the rclean process (improvements to spatial ICA) for all 3T and 7T fMRI data.  These fMRI data are processed similarly to HCP Lifespan data have been released in the HCP-YA 2025 release on ConnectomeDB powered by BALSA (see ConnectomeDB tab).

### MSMAll pipeline
MSMAll (MSM-All) registration uses information on areal features derived from T1w/T2w myelin maps, Resting State Network (RSN) maps, and rfMRI visuotopic maps in a joint multi-modal surface registration to align a subject's cortical data to a group template for improved intersubject registration of functional cortical areas. MSMAll is run on the cleaned grayordinate-wise timeseries after Functional Preprocessing and sICA+FIX processing (single run or multi-run). This pipeline produces output files similar to MSMSulc outputs using this additional registration (denoted with “MSMAll” in the file names to distinguish from FS [FreeSurfer] and MSMSulc outputs). 

The motivation for adding this registration to the structural preprocessing pipeline comes from the observation that while registration based on cortical folding patterns (as is done by MSMSulc in the PostFreeSurfer pipeline) performs well in early areas such as V1 in the calcarine sulcus and areas 4, 3a, and 3b in the central sulcus, folding patterns are substantially less correlated with association areas implicated in ‘higher’ levels of processing (e.g. areas 44 and 45 in the inferior frontal gyrus) (Fischl et al., 2008; Van Essen et al., 2005; Van Essen et al., 2012; Coalson et al., 2018). As demonstrated in Robinson et al. 2014, registration using myelin and RSN maps can improve cross-subject alignment of independent tfMRI datasets resulting in sharper maps and improved group tfMRI statistics, while at the same time decreasing the cross-subject alignment of cortical folding patterns that do not correlate with cortical areal locations. In Robinson et al. 2018, the MSMAll method was improved to produce more robust multimodal alignment by improving control over the smoothness of the cortical surface deformation.

### Note: Difference between HCP-YA vs. Lifespan data: 
MSMAll was updated to use strain-based regularization functions instead of the higher order angular deviation-based regularization functions used originally in HCP-YA (Glasser et al., 2016).  

### Temporal ICA (tICA) pipeline
Spatial ICA (as discussed above for the sICA +FIX pipeline) does an excellent job of separating artifacts that have a specific temporal pattern localized to a specific location or locations (e.g., from movement, from vascular pulsation, or from localized MRI equipment-related artifacts). Removing global artifacts, such as from respiration, has presented a more difficult challenge.  sICA is inherently unable to separate global temporal fluctuations into spatially orthogonal signal and noise components and thus the global fluctuations will remain mixed into all of the sICA component timeseries. 

The functional neuroimaging community has long debated the optimal methodological approach for appropriately and comprehensively removing structured (i.e., non-Gaussian) temporal noise from fMRI data. In considering approaches for HCP, we were looking for temporal cleanup methods that selectively remove artifacts while preserving as much of the neurally generated signal of interest as possible. As fMRI acquisition methods have become more advanced, allowing greater temporal resolution and larger numbers of timepoints per subject, temporal ICA has become a feasible analysis strategy (Smith et al., 2012).

The tICA pipeline subdivides the sICA-cleaned data into temporally orthogonal independent components (using tICA) (Glasser et al., 2018; Glasser et al., 2019). Unlike sICA, which is mathematically blind to global signal and artifact, tICA is able to identify and separate global and semi-global temporally independent components (Smith et al., 2012). The objective is to clean the fMRI timeseries of global artifacts from respiratory or other sources so that the fMRI timeseries reflects, as accurately as possible, the “true” neural BOLD signal that occurred in each subject's brain during their scans. Note that the temporal ICA approach is a selective approach (affecting only artifacts and not affecting the neural signal) that renders non-selective denoising approaches such as global signal regression (or other approaches that effectively remove the mean global signal but have other names) duplicative. 

#### Note: Difference between HCP-YA vs. Lifespan data: 
Temporal ICA was not initially applied to the HCP-YA data but has been applied to the HCP-YA 2025 release data.  
